name = "$(profile_underscored)_$(target)"
description = "$(profile) runtime for the $(pretty_target) SoC"
version = "$(version)"

long-description = """
## Usage

First edit your `alire.toml` file and add the following elements:
 - Add `$(profile_underscored)_$(target)` in the dependency list:
   ```toml
   [[depends-on]]
   $(profile_underscored)_$(target) = "*"
   ```
 - if applicable, apply any runtime configuration variables (see below).

Then edit your project file to add the following elements:
 - "with" the run-time project files. With this, gprbuild will compile the run-time before your application
   ```ada
$(project_file_withs)
   ```
 - Specify the `Target` and `Runtime` attributes:
   ```ada
      for Target use runtime_build'Target;
      for Runtime ("Ada") use runtime_build'Runtime ("Ada");
   ```
 - specify the `Linker` switches:
   ```ada
   package Linker is
     for Switches ("Ada") use Runtime_Build.Linker_Switches;
   end Linker;
   ```

The runtime is configurable via Alire crate configuration variables.
See the project website for full details of the available options.

By default, the runtime is configured for the STM32H723ZG on the NUCLEO-H723ZG
board: an 8 MHz external clock on the HSE input (from the on-board ST-LINK, so
`HSE_Bypass = true`), PLL1 multiplying it by 65 to a 520 MHz CPU clock at
voltage scaling VOS0, the AXI/AHB clock at 260 MHz and the APB clocks at
130 MHz. If you are using a different MCU of the STM32H723/725/730/733/735
line, or a board with a crystal, configure the runtime in your `alire.toml`.
For example, for an STM32H733VG with a 25 MHz crystal:
```toml
[configuration.values]
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).MCU_Sub_Family        = "H733"
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).MCU_Flash_Memory_Size = "G"
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).HSE_Clock_Frequency   = 25000000
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).HSE_Bypass            = false
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).PLL_M_Div             = 5
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).PLL_N_Mul             = 104
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).PLL_P_Div             = 1
```

The runtime generates a compile-time error when the configuration breaks a
limit of the chip: the PLL1 input (1 .. 16 MHz after the M divider), the VCO
(192 .. 836 MHz wide, 150 .. 420 MHz medium), an odd P divider other than 1,
or the CPU, AXI/AHB and APB maxima of the selected voltage scaling
(520 / 275 / 137.5 MHz at VOS0). The flash wait states follow RM0468 Table 16.

The fault stack is 4 KB by default (`Fault_Stack_Size`, bytes, in every profile); the tasking
crates' interrupt stack too (`Interrupt_Stack_Size`, which the light crate refuses):
```toml
[configuration.values]
$(profile_underscored)_$(target).Fault_Stack_Size = 8192
```
Every fault - NMI, hard, memory management, bus, usage - is handled on the fault stack: the fault
trap switches to it before the report, so a fault's handling never runs on the stack that overflowed
and never walks below it. On embedded that handling - the raise in `System.BB.Faults.Report`, the
unwinder and the last-chance handler - needs about 1.8 KB before the application's handler does
anything (1792 bytes measured with gnat_arm_elf 16.1.0, the testcase `test_fault_stack_use`); the
embedded crate refuses a fault stack below 3072 bytes at compile time (the path alone needs 1.8 KB, a handler that
allocates as the default one does 2 KB). An overflow of the interrupt
stack into its guard window is reported as a memory management fault like any other (before the
fault stack, the handler's own first push into the window escalated it to a hard fault reported from
below the stack). A fault inside the handling itself parks the report instead of repeating it. Size
the interrupt stack for the deepest nesting and the guard window.

`Supply_Configuration` is the board's core-supply arrangement, RM0468 Table 30: `LDO` (row 1, the
default: the NUCLEO-H723ZG and every part without the SMPS step-down converter), `SMPS` (row 2, the
core from the converter, LDO bypassed), `SMPS_1V8_Supplies_LDO` / `SMPS_2V5_Supplies_LDO` (row 3),
`SMPS_1V8_Supplies_External_And_LDO` / `SMPS_2V5_Supplies_External_And_LDO` (row 4),
`SMPS_1V8_Supplies_External_LDO_Bypass` / `SMPS_2V5_Supplies_External_LDO_Bypass` (row 5), `Bypass`
(row 6). The converter exists on the STM32H725 and STM32H735 and on the STM32H730 variants with the Q
suffix; the STM32H723 and STM32H733 have none, and the crate refuses the SMPS arrangements for them
(an invalid byte would lock the register until a power cycle). `Bypass`, an external supply with
the LDO off, exists on every part. At VOS0, the default, a converter that feeds the LDO must output
2.5 V (DS13311 Table 14): the crate refuses `SMPS_1V8_Supplies_LDO` and
`SMPS_1V8_Supplies_External_And_LDO` there and accepts them from VOS1 down; the scaling itself is
reachable from every source on this family, the direct converter and bypass included. The start-up
writes the arrangement once into PWR_CR3, before anything touches RAM, and waits for the voltage
levels to be
valid. The register is write-once until a power-on reset: a changed value takes effect after a power
cycle, and an arrangement the board is not wired for leaves the core unsupplied until one. Read the
board's schematic before changing it.

The runtime's data, stacks and heap live in the DTCM; the `.axisram` section is the AXI SRAM. At
start-up the ECC of the AXI SRAM and of the ITCM is initialised word by word, for exactly the sizes
the device's `TCM_AXI_SHARED` option byte gives (64 KB ITCM / 320 KB AXI SRAM by default; 128/256,
192/192 or 256/128 otherwise), so sub-word writes anywhere in them raise no RAMECC flag; the DTCM
needs none (measured); SRAM1, SRAM2 and SRAM4 are the application's to write whole-word before any
sub-word use. The linker scripts describe the default sharing: a device set otherwise has less AXI
SRAM than they declare. **A DMA cannot reach the DTCM**, so a DMA buffer goes in `.axisram` or in
the two sections for the RAMs made for it, `.sram12` (SRAM1 and SRAM2, D2) and `.sram4` (SRAM4, D3;
the only one the BDMA reaches),
through `Linker_Section`; those two carry no initial values, the application enables their clocks
(`RCC_AHB2ENR` for SRAM1/2) and writes them whole-word first. All three are cacheable in the default
memory map: clean the cache before a DMA reads a buffer, keep the buffer's lines clean or invalid
while a DMA writes it, invalidate after the DMA has written, with the barriers of errata ES0491 2.1.4, or give the buffer an MPU region of its own (the stack
guard uses regions 0 to 3; applications start at 4). Text buffers for semihosting output are read by the host behind the
cache as well; the runtime's own live in the DTCM.

`Stack_Guard = true` makes the runtime keep an MPU no-access window of `Stack_Guard_Bytes` at the
bottom of every stack (each task's, the environment task's and the interrupt stack),
re-programmed at each context switch, so that the first write past a stack's bottom is a
memory-management fault reported through the last-chance handler instead of a silent
corruption of the memory below. The window sits inside the stack at its bottom: the interrupt
and environment stacks, which the linker aligns to the window, lose exactly one window; a task's
stack, aligned by the compiler to 8, loses between one and two. **Every guarded stack must be at
least four times `Stack_Guard_Bytes`**: the runtime checks the interrupt stack at compile time and
the environment stack at link time ("environment stack smaller than four guard windows", a
linker assertion against `__stack_size`); a task's `Storage_Size` is the application's, which can state the rule next
to each task:
```ada
pragma Compile_Time_Error
  (Light_Tasking_Stm32h7xx_Config.Stack_Guard
     and then My_Task_Stack_Size < 4 * Light_Tasking_Stm32h7xx_Config.Stack_Guard_Bytes,
   "My_Task's stack is smaller than four guard windows");
```
The two static guards - the environment stack's, and the interrupt stack's where one exists - are
programmed by the start-up for every program, tasking or not; the running task's follows once the tasking runtime is
up (a program without a tasking unit has only the environment stack, and it is guarded). The guard uses MPU regions 0 to 3 (the running task's window alternates between 0 and 3, so the outgoing task's stays armed through a context switch); applications using the MPU themselves should start at region 4. Off
by default. `Stack_Guard_Bytes` sets the window: 32 (the default), 64, 128, 256 or 512 (the runtime's idle
task has a 2 KB stack of its own, which holds four of the largest);
a frame whose first write lands more than the window below the previous stack pointer steps
over it, so size the window to the largest frame of code that is not compiled with
`-fstack-check`.

`Interrupt_Stack_Size`, `Stack_Guard` and `Stack_Guard_Bytes` act in the tasking runtimes
only; the light crate refuses them at compile time if set away from their defaults (so
`light_tasking_$(target).Interrupt_Stack_Size = 8192` belongs in a tasking crate's values). The floors:
`Interrupt_Stack_Size` 1024 bytes, `Fault_Stack_Size` 512 bytes (3072 on embedded, where the
fault's handling unwinds on it).
"""

authors = ["AdaCore", "Daniel King", "Hyle Systems S.R.L."]
maintainers = ["Daniel King <damaki.gh@gmail.com>"]
maintainers-logins = ["damaki"]
licenses = "GPL-3.0-or-later WITH GCC-exception-3.1"
tags = ["embedded", "runtime", "stm32h7"]
website = "https://github.com/damaki/community-bb-runtimes"

project-files = $(project_files_list)

[configuration]
generate_c = false
output_dir = "gnat_user"

[configuration.variables]
MCU_Sub_Family = { type = "Enum", values = ["H723", "H725", "H730", "H733", "H735"], default = "H723" }
MCU_Flash_Memory_Size = { type = "Enum", values = ["B", "E", "G"], default = "G" }

HSE_Clock_Frequency = { type = "Integer", first = 4000000, last = 50000000, default = 8000000 }
HSE_Bypass = { type = "Boolean", default = true }
HSI_Div = { type = "Enum", values = ["DIV1", "DIV2", "DIV4", "DIV8"], default = "DIV1" }

PLL_Src = { type = "Enum", values = ["HSE", "HSI"], default = "HSE" }
PLL_M_Div = { type = "Integer", first = 1, last = 63, default = 1 }
PLL_N_Mul = { type = "Integer", first = 4, last = 512, default = 65 }
PLL_P_Div = { type = "Integer", first = 1, last = 128, default = 1 }
PLL_Q_Div = { type = "Integer", first = 1, last = 128, default = 4 }
PLL_R_Div = { type = "Integer", first = 1, last = 128, default = 2 }
PLL_Q_Enable = { type = "Boolean", default = false }
PLL_R_Enable = { type = "Boolean", default = false }

SYSCLK_Src = { type = "Enum", values = ["HSI", "CSI", "HSE", "PLL1"], default = "PLL1" }
Voltage_Scaling = { type = "Enum", values = ["VOS0", "VOS1", "VOS2", "VOS3"], default = "VOS0" }

D1CPRE = { type = "Enum", values = ["DIV1", "DIV2", "DIV4", "DIV8", "DIV16", "DIV64", "DIV128", "DIV256", "DIV512"], default = "DIV1" }
HPRE = { type = "Enum", values = ["DIV1", "DIV2", "DIV4", "DIV8", "DIV16", "DIV64", "DIV128", "DIV256", "DIV512"], default = "DIV2" }
D1PPRE = { type = "Enum", values = ["DIV1", "DIV2", "DIV4", "DIV8", "DIV16"], default = "DIV2" }
D2PPRE1 = { type = "Enum", values = ["DIV1", "DIV2", "DIV4", "DIV8", "DIV16"], default = "DIV2" }
D2PPRE2 = { type = "Enum", values = ["DIV1", "DIV2", "DIV4", "DIV8", "DIV16"], default = "DIV2" }
D3PPRE = { type = "Enum", values = ["DIV1", "DIV2", "DIV4", "DIV8", "DIV16"], default = "DIV2" }

Supply_Configuration = { type = "Enum", values = ["LDO", "SMPS", "SMPS_1V8_Supplies_LDO", "SMPS_2V5_Supplies_LDO", "SMPS_1V8_Supplies_External_And_LDO", "SMPS_2V5_Supplies_External_And_LDO", "SMPS_1V8_Supplies_External_LDO_Bypass", "SMPS_2V5_Supplies_External_LDO_Bypass", "Bypass"], default = "LDO" }
Interrupt_Stack_Size = { type = "Integer", first = 1024, default = 4096 }
Fault_Stack_Size = { type = "Integer", first = 512, default = 4096 }
Stack_Guard = { type = "Boolean", default = false }
Stack_Guard_Bytes = { type = "Integer", first = 32, last = 512, default = 32 }

[[depends-on]]
gnat_arm_elf = "^16"
